Perspektivism holds that all judgments – including moral ones – are made from particular points of view and that there are no “objective” ethical absolutes.  As the passage on perspectivism explains, it “rejects objective metaphysics… there are no ethical or epistemological absolutes” and “‘Truth’ is thus created by integrating different vantage points together.”  Because moral standards are therefore tied to the perspectives of individuals or cultures, perspectivism resembles the relativist side of the ethics debate, which “does not believe that there are self‑evident moral principles that are true for everyone” and sees moral judgments as shaped by customs and context.  

At the same time, Richard Schacht’s interpretation of Nietzsche suggests that a revised form of objectivity can arise when many singular viewpoints are aggregated: each perspective “adds to the overall objective measure of a proposition under examination.”  In this sense, perspectivism allows a composite moral view to be built from multiple perspectives, even while it denies any single, absolute moral truth.  

So, according to the provided context, perspectivism relates to moral standards and ethics by:

* denying universal, absolute moral facts;
* treating moral judgments as perspective‑dependent;
* allowing a more comprehensive ethical understanding to emerge when diverse perspectives are integrated.
